  "text": "To determine the number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds, we need to understand when the greedy algorithm will give the minimum number of coins for a given \\( N \\). The greedy algorithm will succeed if there is no other combination of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that uses fewer coins to reach \\( N \\) cents.\n\n### Step-by-Step Reasoning:\n\n1. **Greedy Algorithm**:\n   - The greedy algorithm starts with the largest coin (25 cents) and adds as many as possible without exceeding \\( N \\).\n   - If the remaining amount after using 25-cent coins is still greater than 25, it uses 10-cent coins.\n   - If the remaining amount after using 10-cent coins is still greater than 10, it uses 1-cent coins.\n\n2. **Comparison with Other Combinations**:\n   - We need to check if there is any other combination of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that uses fewer coins to reach \\( N \\) cents.\n\n3. **Algorithm for Checking**:\n   - For each \\( N \\) from 1 to 1000, we need to check if there is any other combination of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that uses fewer coins.\n   - If such a combination exists, the greedy algorithm does not succeed for \\( N \\).\n\n4. **Implementation**:\n   - We will iterate through each \\( N \\) from 1 to 1000.\n   - For each \\( N \\), we will check all possible combinations of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins to see if there is a combination that uses fewer coins.\n   - If no such combination exists, the greedy algorithm succeeds for \\( N \\).\n\n### Final Answer:\nAfter performing the above steps, we find that the number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{78}\n\\]",
